      {
        "pkcontributionId": 73291,
        "bookId": 45,
        "chapterId": 176,
        "headingId": 501,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>None</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2021-09-30T16:19:05"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 523,
    "bookid": 45,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Localization",
    "headingPosition": 6,
    "headingid": 502,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 73292,
        "bookId": 45,
        "chapterId": 176,
        "headingId": 502,
        "headingText": "<p>Juvenile xanthogranuloma of the CNS localizes to the brain (in 53% of cases), intradural extramedullary spine (in 13%), or nerve roots (in 15%), with meningeal involvement also being common {22640164}.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2021-10-04T15:18:42"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 524,
    "bookid": 45,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 503,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 73293,
        "bookId": 45,
        "chapterId": 176,
        "headingId": 503,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Tumour lesions induce location-related neurological symptoms, whereas the rare neurodegenerative-like lesions induce more diffuse symptoms including cognitive disturbances {31685033}. On MRI, tumour lesions appear isointense to hyperintense on T1-weighted images, with homogeneous contrast enhancement, and neurodegenerative-like lesions include non-enhancing hyperintense T2 signals and atrophy {31685033; 25281434}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2021-09-30T16:19:05"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 525,
    "bookid": 45,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 504,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 73294,
        "bookId": 45,
        "chapterId": 176,
        "headingId": 504,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>CNS juvenile xanthogranuloma typically occurs in children and young adults {31685033; 22640164; 27173098}. Neurological involvement is seen in &lt;&nbsp;5% of patients with cutaneous juvenile xanthogranuloma.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2021-09-30T16:19:05"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 526,
    "bookid": 45,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 505,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 73295,
        "bookId": 45,
        "chapterId": 176,
        "headingId": 505,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Unknown</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2021-09-30T16:19:05"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 527,
    "bookid": 45,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 506,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 73296,
        "bookId": 45,
        "chapterId": 176,
        "headingId": 506,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The frequency of <em>BRAF</em> p.V600E mutations is currently unclear. <em>ARAF</em> mutations occur in 18% of cases. <em>KRAS</em> and <em>NRAS</em> mutations are also frequent (in as many as 20% of cases) {31768065; 26566875}. One study found that occasional cases can have combined <em>NRAS</em> and <em>ARAF</em> mutations {26566875}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2021-09-30T16:19:05"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 534,
    "bookid": 45,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 513,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 73297,
        "bookId": 45,
        "chapterId": 176,
        "headingId": 513,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Juvenile xanthogranuloma lesions are often received as fragmented, soft, yellow to tan-pink biopsy specimens.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2021-09-30T16:19:05"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 528,
    "bookid": 45,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 507,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 73298,
        "bookId": 45,
        "chapterId": 176,
        "headingId": 507,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Juvenile xanthogranuloma (overlapping with Erdheim&ndash;Chester disease) is composed of rounded to spindled, variably vacuolated histiocytes, scattered Touton and foreign body&ndash;type giant cells, lymphocytes, and occasional eosinophils {31685033}.</p>\r\n<h5>Immunophenotype</h5>\r\n<p>The neoplastic histiocytes of juvenile xanthogranuloma are CD1a-negative, CD11c-positive, CD14-positive, CD68-positive, factor&nbsp;XIIIa&ndash;positive, lysozyme-negative, and S100-negative. BRAF p.V600E protein is present in mutant cases {31685033}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2021-09-30T16:19:05"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 529,
    "bookid": 45,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 508,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 73299,
        "bookId": 45,
        "chapterId": 176,
        "headingId": 508,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2021-09-30T16:19:05"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 530,
    "bookid": 45,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Diagnostic molecular pathology",
    "headingPosition": 14,
    "headingid": 509,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 73300,
        "bookId": 45,
        "chapterId": 176,
        "headingId": 509,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Testing for potentially mutant genes (<em>BRAF</em>, <em>ARAF</em>, <em>KRAS</em>, and <em>NRAS</em>) should be performed with sensitive assays, reflecting the frequently low proportion of neoplastic cells. Mutations of <em>CSF1R</em> and fusions involving an NTRK gene have been reported for peripheral juvenile xanthogranuloma, so their assessment may be particularly useful in cases not limited to the CNS. For NTRK fusions, RNA sequencing or in&nbsp;situ hybridization can be applied, depending on the tumour cell density.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2021-09-30T16:19:05"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 533,
    "bookid": 45,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Essential and desirable diagnostic criteria",
    "headingPosition": 15,
    "headingid": 512,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 73301,
        "bookId": 45,
        "chapterId": 176,
        "headingId": 512,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>See <a style=\"cursor:pointer\" href=\"/attachment/45/176/22837\" target=\"_blank\">#22837</a>Box&nbsp;10.11 .</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2021-09-30T16:19:05"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 531,
